-- 1 ban-van
update public.books set description_en = $mota$Across more than 200 pages and 49 pieces, writer Nguyễn Quang Lập offers his own take on his “literary friends”: Xuân Diệu, Trần Dần, Bảo Sinh, Trọng Đài, Nguyễn Khải, Trần Vàng Sao, Nguyễn Trọng Tạo, Trần Đăng Khoa, Hoàng Phủ Ngọc Tường, Nguyễn Minh Châu, Bảo Ninh, Xuân Sách, Bùi Giáng, Hoàng Cầm, Phùng Quán… With just a few lines describing a gesture or a favorite catchphrase (“tôốk lắm tôốk lắm”, “âu chầu”, “may chi nỏ”...), readers can sense the “essence” of each artist, and those in literary circles will burst out laughing as they recognize their friend’s quirks in Nguyễn Quang Lập’s playful, joking, yet quietly moving voice.$mota$, description_auto_translated = true
where id = 1 and language = 'vi' and description_en is null and md5(description) = '35a5586e3030e5f23e04c645cfac63c4';

-- 4 cam-thu-quan
update public.books set description_en = $mota$Writers borrow historical settings for many reasons: some know that history sells, some use old tales to speak about the present, some are enchanted by the atmosphere of vermilion palaces and gilded chambers… In the end, though, what matters in creative writing is the message the writer wants to convey. With that purpose in mind, the writer chooses a backdrop against which the characters can freely pour out their hearts and struggle with their fate. “Cầm Thư quán” is set in the most prosperous period of the Early Lê dynasty, under the reign of King Lê Thánh Tông, in contrast with a longing to break free of every ritual constraint and every bond of social rank in pursuit of spiritual freedom. Although she borrows historical characters and details, Hà Thuỷ Nguyên does not write a historical novel, but a story about the pursuit of beauty and freedom. “Cầm Thư quán” was first published in 2008 by the Women’s Publishing House; now, ten years later, the book “returns to the world” with a new look, presented by Book Hunter under license from the Writers’ Association Publishing House. Hà Thuỷ Nguyên has polished the prose and revised the verse translations and the quotations from Zhuangzi’s “Nam Hoa Kinh” (Zhuangzi). In addition, she has written a long poem titled “Hồ Khúc” to serve as a preface. This 2018 edition of “Cầm Thư quán” is the most complete version, quite different from the ebooks currently circulating on the Internet. Following Hà Thuỷ Nguyên’s “Cầm Thư quán”, Book Hunter will introduce, one by one, literary works from Vietnam and abroad to readers who love aesthetic prose and free thinking. Book Hunter is a group of young writers and translators working online who share the goal of promoting scholarship and creativity in Vietnam. Beginning with “Cầm Thư quán”, we hope to offer readers an experience of East Asian beauty: hazy, drifting and profound, with a sadness as vast as the sea.$mota$, description_auto_translated = true
where id = 4 and language = 'vi' and description_en is null and md5(description) = '53da08021c2404830881c5285289dd0f';

