-- 7 muoi-chin-chan-dung-nha-van-cung-thoi
update public.books set description_en = $mota$“These are major figures of modern Vietnamese literature such as Ngô Tất Tố, Vũ Trọng Phụng, Nguyễn Tuân, Nam Cao, Nguyễn Bính, Tô Hoài... These works are especially precious and valuable in many respects, and to understand them one must place them within the whole body of Vũ Bằng’s memoirs and recollections. The literary portraits are written from memories, each focused on a different individual: fellow writers and artists of his time, portraits held in memory. Unlike the portraits by some other authors, who wrote about contemporaries still living, sharing the same place and the same unified literary scene (what might be called portraits drawn from life), Vũ Bằng’s portraits, though about his contemporaries, mostly depict people who had already passed away, or who, if still alive, lived completely cut off on the other side of the North–South divide; they are necessarily memoir portraits... For Vũ Bằng, a writer is also a human being, and every human being has a good side and a bad side, strengths and flaws: “People are not saints, so how could they be perfect?” (Phong Di Vũ Đình Long, the immortal in the Tân Dân cave), especially those for whom “talent comes with vices”, as if by fate, as a universal rule, as though Heaven never gives anyone anything whole (Nguyễn Tuân: the pampered child of Angel and Devil; The talent and the vices of Vũ Trọng Phụng; Talent and vice in Tản Đà...). From this view of writers, further deepened by the eye of a friend, the portraits emerge as “ordinary people” (his own words in the piece In Memory of a Master: Quan Thành Nguyễn Văn Vĩnh), with details of everyday life… This is life’s truest face. Yet Vũ Bằng also understood more deeply than anyone that, ordinary as they were, they were not like just any ordinary people, but ordinary people bound to the calling of literature, the kind of people whose “destiny and being are lit by the literary star”. Vũ Bằng perceives and renders each portrait in two capacities at once, as ordinary person and as artist (the commoner and the man of letters), the two always intertwined and hard to separate. And so, from a vivid and abundant store of memories, the material comes back to life, giving flesh, bone and blood to the portraits that rise from the page. …Vũ Bằng’s literary portraits help readers understand and clearly feel the literary situation and the spirit of the times before 1945, from then until 1954 in the North, and after 1954 in urban Saigon. Through Vũ Bằng and a few other figures, we see more than ever the need for a comprehensive history of twentieth-century literature of a unified Vietnam...”$mota$, description_auto_translated = true
where id = 7 and language = 'vi' and description_en is null and md5(description) = '69963bb687f830dd96797795151a4f91';

-- 9 van-thi-si-tien-chien
update public.books set description_en = $mota$Guided by the principle of “speaking only of the literary friends I had the honor of knowing well”, poet Nguyễn Vỹ sketches portraits of pre-war writers, helping readers better understand the “generation of Early Modern Literature”, including Tản Đà, Nguyễn Văn Vĩnh, Phạm Quỳnh, Vũ Trọng Phụng, Lê Văn Trương, Lan Khai, Lưu Trọng Lư, Vi Huyền Đắc, Khái Hưng, Nhất Linh, Thế Lữ, Trương Tửu, Mộng Sơn, Nguyễn Tuân, Phạm Huy Thông, Nguyễn Nhược Pháp, Anh Thơ, Vũ Bằng, Thâm Tâm, Vũ Ngọc Phan, Trần Tuấn Khải, Phan Khôi, Thiếu Sơn, Đông Hồ, Mộng Tuyết, Quách Tấn, Chế Lan Viên, Hàn Mặc Tử… “This work is neither a literary history nor a scholarly study. It is the testimony of an era, by someone who walked the eager path of the Early Modern Literature generation and knocked about every day with his fellow travelers. It lived, it saw, it felt, in the midst of a new world that suddenly emerged from an old one. It shared the glories and humiliations of the writer’s lot.” (Nguyễn Vỹ) Through Văn thi sĩ tiền chiến, readers meet a Nguyễn Vỹ who was disciplined, orderly and rigorous in his work, yet disliked staying within worn-out conventions and was always searching for new forms of expression in his writing. People who met him briefly found him somewhat cold and proud, but with close friends he was warm and sincere. “What distinguishes this work is that it is a ‘testimony of the Early Modern Literature era’, since Nguyễn Vỹ was a writer and poet already famous before the war, who ‘lived, saw and felt’ that very era. It is a captivating memoir about writers and poets, told by the author himself (Chapter I), about their spiritual and material lives and the atmosphere of pre-war arts and letters (Chapters II and III). These are all ‘living’, practical documents that help us understand pre-war arts and letters more deeply and broadly, and a valuable contribution to the history of modern Vietnamese literature.”$mota$, description_auto_translated = true
where id = 9 and language = 'vi' and description_en is null and md5(description) = '12f350f3e4566ff4309bb24c1a1ec9e5';

