-- 11 khac-hoa-buc-tranh-lich-su-nam-ky
update public.books set description_en = $mota$Drawing on rich historical sources and nearly 150 illustrations, some published here for the first time, “Khắc họa bức tranh lịch sử Nam kỳ” offers readers useful insights into Saigon in particular and Cochinchina (Nam kỳ) in general. It recounts the historical events that unfolded in old Nam kỳ from the time of Gia Long, Minh Mạng and Lê Văn Duyệt until the French invaded and established a colonial regime in Nam kỳ and protectorates in Bắc kỳ and Trung kỳ of the kingdom of Đại Nam (today’s Vietnam). In this book, the author portrays the history of Nam kỳ with historical documents and illustrations, through several periods. It begins when Nguyễn Ánh took refuge in Gia Định from the attacks of the Tây Sơn army; there, the French and the people of Gia Định did much to help the Nguyễn lord build his cause, restore his realm and unify the country. Gia Định also gave rise to a challenging local power that King Minh Mạng labored to defuse. In the second half of the 19th century, East Asia in general, and Đại Nam in particular, faced the threat of Western invasion. After opening fire on Đà Nẵng Bay in 1858, the Franco-Spanish forces moved on to attack Nam kỳ, took the citadel of Gia Định, set up the “pagoda line” from Saigon to Chợ Lớn, went on to capture the great fort of Chí Hòa, seized Mỹ Tho… and took all three eastern provinces of Nam kỳ, later adding the three western provinces of Đại Nam. In 1874, the Giáp Tuất Treaty forced the Huế court to cede all six provinces of Nam kỳ to the French. France then forced the Huế court to sign two more treaties in succession, the Quý Mùi Treaty of 1883 and the Giáp Thân Treaty of 1884, turning Bắc kỳ and Trung kỳ of Đại Nam into French protectorates. In 1887, the Indochinese Union was established under a Governor-General, ruling the colony and the two protectorates of Đại Nam as well as the kingdom of Cambodia (Cao Miên); its capital was in Saigon (Nam kỳ) and only moved to Hanoi in 1902. Although no longer the seat of power, Nam kỳ remained the economic capital of the Union, with Saigon as the urban and political center of the colonial government and the cradle of early Vietnamese journalism. The years 1923–1926 saw a wave of migration to Saigon, including many Western-educated intellectuals and well-known political activists, veteran journalists who used their pens to fight and openly confront the colonial government. Saigon was also swept into the world economic crisis a few years later: businesses and traders suffered losses and went bankrupt, the people of Saigon fell on hard times, and streams of people left one after another; Westerners and Chinese residents returned home, while the Vietnamese withdrew to the outskirts of Gò Vấp and Phú Nhuận or went back to their home villages to scrape a living, hoping the Great Depression would soon pass so they could return to Saigon. During the Depression, Saigon’s people held on, waiting for a new normal, and even in those hard times they kept their generous spirit, sharing community responsibility through many forms of charity… Through accounts of the historical events that took place in Nam kỳ, and especially through nearly 150 vivid and valuable pictures and photographs of Nam kỳ, including 24 pages in color, the book offers readers different perspectives on the people and the land, illustrating the events mentioned throughout the book and presenting precious images of old Nam kỳ as seen by Westerners. When reproducing old pictures and photographs, besides captions describing their content, the author also notes whose sketch it was, whose drawing, and who the engraver was… helping to bring to light the names of the artists who preserved images of Vietnam’s past. Part of the Vietnamese History collection of Omega Plus Books.$mota$, description_auto_translated = true
where id = 11 and language = 'vi' and description_en is null and md5(description) = '9191a64f13146f082295fe544ac9343e';

-- 12 buoc-qua-nuoc-mat-tu-khac-truong-thanh
update public.books set description_en = $mota$Marking more than a decade of devotion to writing, Anh Khang presents his new work: Bước qua nước mắt, tự khắc trưởng thành. Anh Khang calls it a “book for making peace” rather than a “book for healing”, the term so common in modern society. The book is divided into two parts. It opens with “Monologue”, solitary musings that soothe “the inner child throwing a tantrum on the road to growing up”, and moves on to “Dialogue”, heartfelt words shared with Gen Z and “Gen Z(à)” in search of comfort. Every line, every whispered confidence is deeply sincere; as the author himself puts it, these are “notes written while ‘having a good long cry’, and from now on, looking only forward”. We hope that in its pages you will find not only nods of agreement and answers to the romantic dilemmas of our clumsy younger years, but also a chance to look back and smile tenderly at our own youth.$mota$, description_auto_translated = true
where id = 12 and language = 'vi' and description_en is null and md5(description) = 'aa38416a33f93857302ee1681e913226';

-- 13 ngon-ngu-tuyen-tap-truyen-ngan-chon-loc-2025-hardcover
update public.books set description_en = $mota$Selected Short Stories, volume two, 2025: submissions were called for early but the book came out late, because several thick “author and works” volumes had to be printed one after another, and alongside the regular magazine there was also a special issue. We hope the authors who sent their work early will not be annoyed with us. Please see it simply as a pastime; sometimes we all need to slow down a little. Thanks to this delay, the book has gained a few more writers whose names have long flourished in Vietnamese letters. Along the path of “Ngôn Ngữ”, we do not hang out a sign of “reconciliation”, but we always invite all of us, those at home and those overseas, to sit together in one book, with complete freedom of subject chosen by each writer, bound only by the same genre: the “short story”. We repeat this constraint in order to answer the few pieces that could not be included because they leaned toward research or genealogy-like records... Our sincere apologies. As with the first volume, each author made their own selection. For our part, if on reading we found no writing heavy with attacking, distorting or eulogizing political tones, nothing borrowed from others (admittedly hard to check, so we rely on each person’s integrity), and the story was readable, we put it on the page. Naturally, with so many pieces, truly fine stories sit beside less outstanding ones; that is only normal. ... In this opening letter we offer no comment on the book’s contents as a whole. Readers and writers will gradually discover its pleasures for themselves. We hope so. Prose and short stories still sell, but as with the previous book (printed one copy at a time with today’s technology in the United States), not many copies can be printed. Running a business selling Vietnamese-language books in America is still a dream. Frankly, we only dare hope that the title will be listed in advertisements abroad, and that copies will be given to libraries to keep. All greater hopes we leave to the next generation. Our efforts are only to find joy in the doing itself (calling for submissions, gathering, reading, editing, laying out, sending to print, and so on). Thank you to all the authors at home and abroad for your understanding, and for giving us the chance to keep breathing the air of the Vietnamese script. Warmly, Luân Hoán, May 9, 2025$mota$, description_auto_translated = true
where id = 13 and language = 'vi' and description_en is null and md5(description) = 'ddc8b8249813af6ce423688e52073d74';

