-- 15 ky-mong
update public.books set description_en = $mota$Nguyễn Du helped make Vietnamese literature a part of the finest of human culture, leaving his mark on the international poetry scene. For The Tale of Kiều in particular, and for his whole body of work in general, generations of Vietnamese have honored Nguyễn Du as the nation’s Great Poet. The World Peace Council named him a World Cultural Figure. In this picture book, Ký Mộng, we are pleased to present to readers excerpts from The Tale of Kiều, the Funeral Oration for All Souls (Văn tế thập loại chúng sinh) and several poems originally written in classical Chinese, translated by poet Đỗ Trung Lai, together with illustrations by artist Niayu inspired by the Great Poet’s works.$mota$, description_auto_translated = true
where id = 15 and language = 'vi' and description_en is null and md5(description) = 'b0458abd3d013d71e27d82212e27dcc6';

-- 16 truyen-kieu
update public.books set description_en = $mota$Đoạn trường tân thanh, better known as Truyện Kiều (The Tale of Kiều), is a verse narrative by the great poet Nguyễn Du. It is regarded as the most famous verse tale and a classic of Vietnamese literature, written in Nôm script in lục bát (six-eight) meter, with 3,254 lines. The story is based on the novel Kim Vân Kiều truyện by Thanh Tâm Tài Nhân, a writer of the Ming dynasty in China. Vương Thúy Kiều is a young woman of perfect talent and beauty. She comes from a middle-class family and has two younger siblings, Thúy Vân and Vương Quan. During the Thanh minh festival, Kiều and her siblings go to tend the family graves. There she meets Kim Trọng, a young man of “graceful bearing, rare talent and looks / refined within and gallant without”. When they meet, “their hearts already seemed to agree, though their faces were still shy”. On the same occasion, Kiều comes upon a desolate grave and, moved, weeps over the fate of the courtesan buried there. Back home, the spirit of Đạm Tiên foretells the storms of her life. After meeting Thúy Kiều at the Thanh minh festival, Kim Trọng seeks news of her, moves to a house near hers and finds a way to get acquainted. While her parents are away, Kiều visits Kim Trọng to share her heart. Their love blossoms; they love and deeply respect each other. They swear vows and exchange tokens of betrothal. Then Kim Trọng’s family is in mourning and he must return to his hometown for the funeral. Meanwhile, Kiều’s father is falsely accused by a silk merchant. Her family is ruined, and Old Vương and Vương Quan are tortured. Kiều must sell herself to ransom her father. Before leaving with Mã Giám Sinh, she asks Thúy Vân to marry Kim Trọng in her place so as not to betray his love. But the tragedy does not end there. Mã Giám Sinh and Tú Bà turn out to be human traffickers. In the brothel, she tries to take her own life but fails. Later, Kiều is deceived by Sở Khanh and bitterly accepts a life of humiliation. Not long after, she is rescued by Thúc Sinh. But Thúc Sinh already has a wife, Hoạn Thư, back home. Learning of her husband’s affair, Hoạn Thư secretly has Thúy Kiều kidnapped and made a servant, then stages a scene forcing Kiều to wait on the couple at a drinking party. Cruelly punished by jealousy, Kiều flees to take refuge in a Buddhist temple. Unfortunately, the abbess unknowingly entrusts her to Bạc Hạnh, a trafficker like Tú Bà, and Kiều falls into a brothel once again. There she meets Từ Hải, a hero who fears neither heaven nor earth. Từ Hải rescues Kiều from the brothel and helps her repay kindness and avenge wrongs. Through a moment of carelessness, Kiều is deceived by Hồ Tôn Hiến, an imperial official, and causes Từ Hải to die standing on the battlefield. Hồ Tôn Hiến then forces her to serve wine all night and marries her off to a local chieftain. On the way, in the bridal palanquin, she throws herself into the Tiền Đường River. Fortunately, she is saved by the nun Giác Duyên. Although married to Thúy Vân, Kim Trọng still longs for Kiều and goes to great lengths to find her. By good fortune, he meets the nun Giác Duyên. Kiều’s family is reunited. To please everyone, Thúy Kiều renews her bond with Kim Trọng, but the two vow to “turn the love of husband and wife into the companionship of music and chess”. ADDITIONAL INFORMATION About the original: – Original language: Nôm script – First published: 1820 – 3,254 lines of lục bát verse. Special features: This edition uses the annotations and textual variants of Assoc. Prof. Vũ Ngọc Khánh, a Vietnamese cultural researcher with many notable studies of folk culture. It also features distinctive illustrations by artist Ngô Mạnh Quỳnh, drawn and engraved.$mota$, description_auto_translated = true
where id = 16 and language = 'vi' and description_en is null and md5(description) = '8902bae5ee3a86a71f4d6a7caf5e9470';

-- 17 bi-quyet-xay-dung-va-quan-tri-thanh-cong-chuoi-cua-hang-dich-vu-sua-chua-xe-may
update public.books set description_en = $mota$We are pleased to introduce “Bí quyết xây dựng và quản trị thành công chuỗi cửa hàng dịch vụ sửa chữa xe máy”. The book was written by author, engineer, manager and trainer Nguyễn Hồng Cương, who has extensive hands-on experience in car and motorbike services and has made a positive difference in the lives of thousands of Vietnamese mechanics and many car and motorbike service businesses. The author writes in plain, accessible language for every kind of reader. What sets the book apart is its practical, “show you step by step” approach, with concrete examples, many templates, and vivid, highly practical visuals that help readers easily apply the knowledge to their daily work. In Vietnam, the motorbike is still the most common means of transport. According to statistics up to the end of 2014, more than 43 million motorbikes had been registered nationwide. In big cities such as Hà Nội and Hồ Chí Minh City, there is on average one motorbike for every two people. This is a big opportunity for the motorbike repair industry. With such numbers, the demand for motorbike care, maintenance and repair is enormous. That is why motorbike repair and maintenance services have sprung up everywhere, from small lanes and alleys to major streets. In reality, however, these services have mushroomed without order, and in general the quality of service at these shops does not yet meet customers’ needs in many respects. Professional organization is still weak and has not earned customers’ trust, which keeps these businesses unstable and slow to grow. We believe this book will help shop owners build their reputation and find management methods that make their service business more effective, so that customers receive honest service worth what they pay. Although written for motorbike services, the book contains overall business management principles that the author has carefully researched and successfully applied in many motorbike and car service businesses. It will be an operating handbook not only for the motorbike service industry; you can apply it to many other kinds of service business. We are pleased to recommend it! Information and Communications Publishing House$mota$, description_auto_translated = true
where id = 17 and language = 'vi' and description_en is null and md5(description) = '0d08b417893c7f13afd075f156b16ffa';

-- 18 nghe-thuat-song
update public.books set description_en = $mota$The art of living is the skill of mastering one’s way of life. A way of life shows in our behavior and manners, which are the result of habits shaped by education or personal experience. When we master our way of life, our words, thoughts and actions, even if they do not directly bring joy to others, never cause harm to anyone, anywhere. This book covers the skills and art of mastering one’s desires, the art of spending money, and how to live in harmony amid disagreement. By grasping the art of living, we will be happier. The art of living presented in this book helps practitioners remove the obstacles and challenges on the path of building a career and studying the Buddha’s teachings, so as to find happiness in the present moment, here and now.$mota$, description_auto_translated = true
where id = 18 and language = 'vi' and description_en is null and md5(description) = '700e6385ed4492c85527c64f3464b609';

-- 19 cach-mang-phap-va-tam-ly-hoc-cua-cac-cuoc-cach-mang
update public.books set description_en = $mota$This is one of Le Bon’s three classic books: The Psychology of Crowds; The Psychological Laws of the Evolution of Peoples; and The French Revolution and the Psychology of Revolutions. “The history of the French Revolution is in fact made up of a series of parallel and often independent historical events: the history of a decaying old regime collapsing for lack of defenders; the history of the revolutionary assemblies; the history of popular movements and their leaders; the history of the armies; the history of new institutions… All these historical stages, which mostly reflect the conflict of psychological forces, must be studied with methods borrowed from the science of psychology.” Every statesman should study Gustave Le Bon’s book. This author was not known for any of the classic theories of the French Revolution, and so his psychological interpretations led him to strikingly new conclusions.$mota$, description_auto_translated = true
where id = 19 and language = 'vi' and description_en is null and md5(description) = '91110da654b9be93e5cc0e3164662e7c';

-- 21 truyen-kieu-tu-ke
update public.books set description_en = $mota$Truyện Kiều (The Tale of Kiều) is a masterpiece of Vietnamese literature. Nguyễn Du breathed life into its characters and portrayed them in masterful language, making them all true to life, with a vitality that reaches beyond the bounds of space and time. For two hundred years, Truyện Kiều has not only lived on in poetry; each of its characters has stepped off the page into the Vietnamese way of thinking and speaking, woven into every breath and heartbeat of the Vietnamese people. Over many decades, Truyện Kiều has inspired every form of art. But what do the characters of this verse tale think? Who are they, really? If they lived today, what would they want and what would they say? Those questions are partly answered in Truyện Kiều tự kể. Sharp confessions and beautiful illustrations give us a more multifaceted and engaging view of this classic, so that, in the spirit of a new age, we come to love Truyện Kiều and Nguyễn Du all the more.$mota$, description_auto_translated = true
where id = 21 and language = 'vi' and description_en is null and md5(description) = 'a0d263a609c63c9723a24bb54ceca7f5';

-- 22 tuyen-tap-van-hoc-viet-cho-thieu-nhi-to-hoai-1-truyen-dong-thoai-kich
update public.books set description_en = $mota$Mainly presents short stories and novellas whose characters and main subjects are animals. Alongside works that have been reprinted many times, it also introduces several of the writer’s “rare, hard-to-find” stories: Con dế mèn, the “forerunner” of the novel “Dế Mèn phiêu lưu ký” (Diary of a Cricket), and a few stories first published in the magazine Truyền bá of Tân Dân Publishing House in 1941–1942 that we have only recently collected: Bốn con chó, Bốn con nỡm ấy đi du lịch… Continuing the fables, the Plays section presents two of the writer’s warm-hearted plays, Con mèo lười and Tích tịch tình tang. Tô Hoài (27.9.1920 – 6.7.2014) is one of Vietnam’s greatest modern writers. He left a rich and varied literary legacy, with outstanding works that have won over readers of many generations, regions and ages. In writing for children alone, Tô Hoài can be called a leading author. Over more than seventy years of writing, he gave childhood many remarkable “gifts”. Based on the list of works the writer recorded during his lifetime and entrusted to Kim Đồng Publishing House; on the form, subjects and genres of his published works; and on some newly collected materials, we have compiled and present, on the 100th anniversary of his birth, Tô Hoài’s Collected Writings for Children. The set has 4 volumes: Volume 1: Fables – Plays; Volume 2: Everyday Life Stories; Volume 3: Stories of Revolutionary Heroes; Volume 4: One Hundred Fairy Tales – Tales of Old.$mota$, description_auto_translated = true
where id = 22 and language = 'vi' and description_en is null and md5(description) = 'd8ab077f8175f2239a00a20a66f9e176';

-- 23 animation-artist
update public.books set description_en = $mota$The 4.0 era has brought sweeping changes to production and daily life, with a considerable impact on how occupations are shifting across society. Faced with so many career choices, how can young Vietnamese keep up with the times, unlock their inner potential, build a “personal brand” amid the fierce competition of the job market, and make well-considered choices as they set out to build their lives and careers? This series follows experts in each field to “lift the curtain” on the behind-the-scenes world of professions booming in the 4.0 era, helping you understand a job so you can do it well. With a “career map” firmly in hand, you will be able to make the right choices for your future and be ready to take great leaps forward to succeed in whatever career you choose. Animation artists are the artists and specialists who design and develop an animated film. In Animation Artist you will discover: the history and future of animation around the world and in Vietnam; the artists in charge of each stage of making an animated work; skills for using animation tools; and where to learn animation and how to win over employers. DeeDee Animation Studio is an international-standard 2D animation studio with branches in Hà Nội and Hồ Chí Minh City. DeeDee has co-produced with leading animation studios worldwide such as Walt Disney Animation, Shin-Ei Animation, TMS Entertainment and 6 Point Harness. Animation Artist – Vén Màn Hậu Trường Của Những Bước Chân 4.0 is the next book in Wings Books’ HƯỚNG NGHIỆP 4.0 (Career Guidance 4.0) series.$mota$, description_auto_translated = true
where id = 23 and language = 'vi' and description_en is null and md5(description) = 'faf6a6c676a5eab87256ddf972f30dcc';

-- 24 cao-thom-lan-gio-quyen-2
update public.books set description_en = $mota$“At the age of one hundred, Hữu Ngọc wished to look back and reflect on his own life, on life and society, through the lens of the world’s most profound minds. This spiritual pilgrimage is the premise and substance of Cảo thơm lần giở. He introduces the lives and thought of more than 180 great figures of East and West, past and present, from every field: religion, culture, philosophy, science, literature, art, ethics, sociology, history, psychology, political science... This grand sketch sets out the main ideas, doctrines and currents of the world’s cultures through their representative figures, including religious founders such as the Buddha, Jesus and the Prophet Muhammad; philosophers such as Confucius, Socrates, Hegel, Sartre...; scientists such as Darwin and Einstein; writers and poets such as Shakespeare, Cervantes, Dante, Goethe, Dostoyevsky, Lu Xun, Tagore, Molière...; statesmen such as Kennedy, Mandela, Obama...; military strategists such as Sun Tzu and Machiavelli...; artists such as Leonardo da Vinci, Picasso, Guitry... From Vietnam there are three figures recognized by UNESCO: Nguyễn Trãi, Nguyễn Du and Hồ Chí Minh. Each figure is presented from two angles: their life, and famous sayings that express their thought. They are arranged alphabetically, giving the book the look of a dictionary. In fact it is not a dictionary in the proper sense, since it lacks the scientific objectivity of a reference work. It can be seen as a deeply personal memoir. First of all, although the figures chosen are representative, the author’s personal tastes and limited research time mean that some important figures are missing. The author hopes to add some of them, who should have been included in this edition, when the book is reprinted. Whim governs the length of each portrait. Thousands of sayings are quoted, touching on different views of the world and of human life. …Readers are invited to join Hữu Ngọc on a cultural journey through time and space, in search of the eternal human being.” (American writer Lady Borton) Cultural scholar Hữu Ngọc in his own words: “The author’s pilgrimage into his own past brought him a little calm to recall private matters as well as the national and international events of his time… While remembering, the author kept wondering about the meaning of past events, and from there reflected on the meaning of human life and human fate… His pilgrimage then took another turn: through books, ‘knocking on the doors’ of the world’s great figures to seek answers to that question. The fruit of that ‘search for masters’ is this book.” Praise: “Never before have I met anyone who bridges cultures like Mr. Hữu Ngọc: deeply rooted in Vietnamese culture, a true patriot, and very sensitive in embracing other cultures.” – Swedish Ambassador Börje Ljunggren (from a speech at the ceremony awarding the Order of the Polar Star at the Swedish Embassy, June 6, 1997) “I have talented and devoted friends and collaborators, foremost among them Hữu Ngọc, a learned man with both knowledge and heart.” – Doctor Nguyễn Khắc Viện (from a speech on receiving the Grand Prize of the Académie française, 1992) “If any intellectual, any writer, deserves this Order of the Academic Palms, it is Mr. Hữu Ngọc, a great man of culture and humanism.” – French Ambassador Claude Blanchemaison (from a speech at the ceremony awarding the Order of the Academic Palms, February 14, 1992) “Mr. Hữu Ngọc is one of Vietnam’s outstanding scholars. In him I salute not only an inexhaustible memory but also a man of refined culture, an image of Vietnam.” – Sylvain, Quebec Minister of International Relations and La Francophonie, Canada (launch of the Quebec-funded book Phác thảo chân dung Hà Nội, November 6, 1997)$mota$, description_auto_translated = true
where id = 24 and language = 'vi' and description_en is null and md5(description) = 'e1c3a4732e09f3ae3bbe2711ab73ffc1';

-- 25 tu-dien-van-hoa-co-truyen-viet-nam
update public.books set description_en = $mota$A dictionary for “strolling through the work, wandering from one entry to the next, leafing through a few pages and turning back, leaping from folk games to scholarly literature, from Taoist rituals to ceremonial dress, from temples and pagodas to ghosts and spirits, from the moon lute to medicinal plants… And perhaps, thanks to this book, one should even lose one’s way in the vast forest of Vietnamese culture.” (Philippe Papin, École française d’Extrême-Orient) “…Từ điển văn hóa cổ truyền Việt Nam was edited by cultural scholar Hữu Ngọc, with collaborators who are specialists and researchers in the fields covered. The book has been published many times, and this edition builds on the 2000 reprint, with “corrections and additions”. Culture is a very broad category, covering almost every aspect of human spiritual and material life. As its title makes clear, the book deals with traditional culture. This means that many contents, concepts and even place names that are no longer common today, or that have been changed or renamed over time, are still presented. Naturally, these include customs and practices that have become outdated (such as certain backward customs) or that run against current views on ecology and the environment (such as ways of hunting some rare animals once common among the people)... At the same time, many changes in the natural, economic and social conditions of localities, provinces and cities over the years have not yet been updated. We hope readers will treat this as a book for understanding the concepts and ideas of traditional culture rather than for looking up specific figures.” – Kim Đồng Publishing House. About the author: Cultural scholar Hữu Ngọc was born on December 22, 1918, and has worked in many fields: teaching, enemy-proselytizing work in the army, cultural diplomacy, writing books, journalism, translation and cultural research... He is fluent in French, English and German and can read classical Chinese. For many decades he has devoted himself to introducing Vietnamese culture abroad and foreign cultures to Vietnam, and friends at home and abroad affectionately call him the “cultural importer and exporter”.$mota$, description_auto_translated = true
where id = 25 and language = 'vi' and description_en is null and md5(description) = '3eff40842272ae25e7ae45e6be8439c1';

